# Migration files keep LF on every platform.
#
# Drizzle records a hash of each migration's text in `drizzle.__drizzle_migrations`
# when it applies it. With `core.autocrlf=true`, which is the Windows default and
# what this machine has, a checkout on Windows and a checkout on the Linux CI
# runner would hand the migrator two different byte sequences for the same file
# and record two different hashes for the same migration.
#
# Nothing reads those hashes back today: Drizzle decides what to run from the
# folder timestamp, not the hash. This is here so that stays a fact about the
# migrator's current implementation rather than something the bookkeeping depends
# on being true.
web/infrastructure/db/migrations/** text eol=lf

# The image's two files keep LF, for a smaller version of the same reason (#531).
#
# A Dockerfile is read by a parser on Linux and its `RUN` lines are handed to a
# shell there. Docker tolerates CRLF today; a shell does not tolerate a stray
# carriage return in the middle of a continued command, and this machine's
# `core.autocrlf` is `true`, so a checkout here would otherwise write CRLF into a
# file that is only ever executed somewhere else.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# The contract and its checker keep LF, for the third version of the same reason
# (#533).
#
# `deploy/contract.json` is copied into the image, and the publish workflow
# compares the copy inside the pushed image against the file in the repository so
# that "this image carries this contract" is checked rather than assumed. That
# comparison is over bytes, and `core.autocrlf` on a Windows checkout would make
# the two differ without either one being wrong. `deploy/check-config.mjs` runs
# inside the image beside it, on Linux.
deploy/** text eol=lf
